## The framing: who makes robots vs. who profits

A useful mental model that most sell-side desks (Morgan Stanley's "Humanoid 100," Goldman, McKinsey) have converged on: the robot *assemblers* (OEMs) are mostly hard to invest in cleanly — they're either private (Figure, Agility, Apptronik, 1X, Unitree pre-IPO) or buried inside giants (Tesla, Xiaomi, BYD). The best near-term liquid opportunity is often in the supply-chain component stocks, the "picks and shovels." That's the core of your "upstream beneficiary" question, and I'll spend the most time there.

