## Key risks to weigh
The whole bull case rests on timing assumptions. The dominant thesis is that AI will improve faster than manufacturing costs fall, opening a 2–4 year window where humanoids beat human labor on cost for more tasks — with the market projected above $50 billion by 2030. But forecasts vary wildly (other estimates put 2030 at $4–18B), production timelines slip routinely, hardware configurations are still changing (so today's "winning" component could be designed out), valuations are stretched, and there are real US-China security/export-control overhangs on Chinese names. Chinese A-shares also carry foreign-access constraints depending on where you trade.

