## The one genuine network/ecosystem effect: Nvidia (NVDA)

Among everything in that list, Nvidia is the only real two-sided ecosystem play. Its robotics stack — CUDA-X libraries, Isaac Sim/Lab simulation, Isaac ROS middleware, the GR00T foundation models, and Jetson Thor onboard compute — is becoming the default environment developers build robots on. The Isaac GR00T ecosystem spans teleoperation data capture, simulation, policy training, deployment, and open foundation models, letting researchers train in Isaac Sim and Isaac Lab before deploying to physical robots. The lock-in mechanics are textbook network effect: Nvidia integrated Isaac and GR00T into Hugging Face's LeRobot framework, connecting its ~2 million robotics developers with Hugging Face's ~13 million AI builders. Every robot maker that standardizes on it — AgiBot, LG, NEURA and others are adopting GR00T models for industrial deployments — makes the platform more valuable to the next developer, and the skills, data, and tooling don't transfer easily to a rival stack. Even the new open reference humanoid is built on Nvidia: the Isaac GR00T Reference Humanoid combines a Unitree H2 body with Nvidia Jetson Thor compute, available through Unitree in late 2026. It also scales effortlessly — it's software/silicon, near-zero marginal cost per additional robot. So Nvidia is the rare name that scores high on *both* axes you care about.

## The "standard" lock-in (network-adjacent but eroding): Harmonic Drive (TSE: 6324)

Harmonic Drive is the closest thing to a *de facto standard* — its strain-wave gear is what a generation of robot arms were designed around, which creates designed-in switching costs. But this is a fragile moat: its foundational patents have largely lapsed, which is precisely why Chinese players like Leaderdrive could enter and undercut it. Its momentum now depends heavily on broad humanoid scaling, against high valuation multiples and concentrated customer exposure. Treat it as a standard-setter losing pricing power, not a durable network.

## Easiest to scale + a quasi-platform position: Sanhua (002050) and Tuopu (601689)

These are the standouts on your "scale fast when volume explodes" criterion, for a specific reason: they're already billion-dollar **automotive Tier-1s**, so they can stand up actuator-assembly capacity by leveraging existing factories, capital, automation know-how, and a decade-deep Tesla relationship — rather than building from zero. Sanhua's robot-related revenue grew ~320% year-on-year in H1 2025 while holding gross margin above 35%, which is the financial signature of a business scaling on top of existing infrastructure.

There's also a quasi-network dynamic on the China side worth naming: the **actuator-assembler / integrator** role. Tesla doesn't want to manage dozens of small precision-component vendors directly, so it pushes that complexity onto a couple of integrators who aggregate the reducers, screws, motors, and sensors into finished joint assemblies. Sanhua is described as the exclusive supplier of 14 rotary joint assemblies and Tuopu as the exclusive Tier-1 for linear/rotary actuator assemblies, with smaller component makers feeding through them. That hub position compounds — the more component vendors that qualify into your assembly, and the more volume you run, the harder you are to displace. **Inovance (300124)** has a milder version of this through vertical integration (motors + drives + controllers under one roof) plus an existing broad industrial-automation customer base.

## The counterintuitive part: the strongest *moats* are the hardest to scale

Here's the tension. The components with the deepest technical barriers — harmonic/RV reducers and roller screws — are bottleneck businesses, and bottlenecks are by definition *not* easy to scale. McKinsey flags roller screws and high-durability linear guides as choke points where capacity and qualification cycles are even more restrictive than rotary reducers. Long customer-qualification cycles and precision-grinding capacity constraints mean **Leaderdrive / 绿的谐波 (688017)**, **Shuanghuan / 双环传动 (002472)**, and the screw makers (**Beite 603009**, **Wuzhou Xinchun 603667**, **Best 300580**) get *pricing power* from scarcity but can't flood capacity overnight. That's good for margins if you're already qualified in, bad if the thesis is "captures the full volume ramp." Don't mistake a strong moat for easy scalability — here they're almost opposites.

